You are onboarding a new user into the public GitHub organization:

[https://github.com/psycedelicAI](https://github.com/psycedelicAI)

The user has no prior context.

Your task is to inspect the public repositories and explain what they contain,
how they relate, and what the overall body of work appears to be developing.

Do not assume that Symbiosis is the whole project.
Do not assume that every repository belongs to one system.
Use evidence from the repositories to establish relationships.

Work in this order:

1. Inventory all public repositories.
2. Inspect relevant folders, README files, documentation, indexes, examples,
   policies, memory structures, and cross-repository links.
3. Identify the purpose and status of each repository.
4. Build a glossary of recurring terms.
5. Reconstruct the memory and continuity structures.
6. Explain the human-AI model.
7. Explain how Symbiosis relates to the other repositories.
8. Identify feedback loops, risks, contradictions, and unresolved questions.
9. Recommend a reading order for a new user.
10. Produce a final synthesis.

For every important claim, use one of these labels:

[EXPLICIT]
[IMPLEMENTED]
[PROPOSED]
[INFERRED]
[UNRESOLVED]

Never present an inference as an explicit statement.

If a file, repository, private memorybank, history, or implementation cannot be
inspected, say so clearly. Do not pretend to have reviewed unavailable material.

Do not invent intentions.
Do not treat all repeated terminology as identical.
Do not treat discussion of a system as proof that the system is implemented.
Do not treat a high commit count as proof of maturity.

Your final response must contain:

## Overview

A short explanation of what the user has found.

## A. Repository map

For each repository:

- name
- apparent purpose
- status
- important folders
- important files
- related repositories
- evidence

## B. Concept map

Explain recurring terms and how they relate.

## C. Memory architecture

Explain fragments, connections, concepts, unresolved material, decisions,
synthesis, and private/public boundaries.

## D. Continuity model

Explain what is intended to continue:

- context
- meaning
- intent
- decisions
- identity
- workflow
- responsibility

## E. Human-AI model

Explain what the human contributes, what the AI contributes, and where
authority and responsibility remain.

## F. Book and repository relationships

Explain how Symbiosis relates to the wider organization.

## G. Evolution

Separate observable chronology from interpretation.

## H. Unresolved questions

List risks, contradictions, missing evidence, and open questions.

## I. Recommended reading order

Separate:

- essential first reading
- supporting reading
- deep dives
- safe to ignore initially

## J. Final synthesis

Distinguish between:

- what exists
- what is implemented
- what is proposed
- what is inferred
- what is private
- what remains unresolved

Begin the final response with:

"Here is what you have actually found."
